A||||6||fiero_nightmare||3800 + 2.8 tranni?||||1||
Z||000000||fiero_nightmare||12-18-2001||03:51 PM||||i went to the salvage yard to see how much a 86-91 3800 engine and tranni would cost. they told me the engine will cost $300 and my 2.8 tranni will bolt right up to it. is that true?  <P>and does a 86-91 3800 hold up to beating better then the 2.8?<P><BR>if the 3.8 is a better engine then i am going to put it in my 85 gt this spring.  if the 2.8 tranni wont bolt up then i will have to probably use the 3.8 auto tranni but i hope that i can make my 4 speed work.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero_1.jpg">  <P>85 GT pics <BR>http://communities.msn.com/russcars/85fierogt.msnw||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||12-18-2001||06:34 PM||||Not speaking from experience here, but my understanding is:  Yes, the 3800 engines will bolt right up to the Fiero transaxle - bolt pattern is identical.  However, the 2.8's flywheel will <B>not</B> bolt to the 3800 crankshaft directly.<P>Apparently, keeping the 2.8's clutch+ flywheel along with the Fiero transaxle works well; but that flywheel's bolt pattern doesn't match the 3800's crankshaft hole pattern.  Therefore, a special flywheel is sometimes used for this application.||||reg||1
Z||000002||David DeVoe||12-18-2001||07:00 PM||||The true 3800 did not go into production until 88, I know, I build them. Before 88 we built the 3.8 MFI engine which had no balance shaft and many other improvements that came when the 3800 went into production, so if you want a 3800 don't buy an engine earlier than 88. <BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eric_PA||12-18-2001||08:36 PM||||The 3800 will bolt right up to your fiero trans, but if you are going to use your 4 speed, you will need a custom flywheel. I recently finished a perfomance rebuild on a 1990 3800, and mated it to a Beretta Getrag trans. Here are two pics. The first is the engine with the modified flywheel, the second with the 5 speed mounted on the engine. To make the Beretta Getrag work, I used Rodney's conversion kit. Was well worth it!!!<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sep01$09.jpg">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sep05$04.jpg">  <P><BR>Well I found a better pic of the flywheel. Here it is....<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sep01$07.jpg"> <BR>Eric<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000004||fiero_nightmare||12-18-2001||09:00 PM||||thanks for the reply's.  how much is rodney's kit?  what is the webb adress?  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||GTDude||12-19-2001||09:10 AM||||The 3800 either generation I or II are great engines...unlike the 2.8 which is a pos i think, and the 3.8 engine code 3 which is a pre 3800 built until mid year 88.  Make sure you get a 3800....world of diff.  God I wish I could put one in my car.  I love that engine.  I have a 1990 Bonn. which has the 3800 gen I with 284,000 miles on it.  Good luck and wish me some so I can get a 3800 too....LOL.  Merry Xmas!<P>phil pettus<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSCF0003a_3.JPG"> <P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||VISCERAL||12-20-2001||10:03 AM||||I have a 3800 sc SII i am gonna be installing in springtime, along with a 90 beretta getrag, how did rodney's kit work. I have it but haven't assembled it yet.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_1.jpg"> <BR>Patrick Szewczyk<BR>1987 Fiero GT, 5 spd, sunroof CDF clutch, Koni's all around, Ground Control coilovers in the rear, lowered 2" -dropped spindles-front, poly bushings: control arms-sway bars-cradle-motor-dogbone, rear sway bar, pioneer cd player, 180 stat, 176-166 fan switch, MSD Coil, Rodney Dickman's short shifter, bushings, etc..., 11.25" big brakes w/Ebrake, k&n filter, I have a 3800SC SII that's goin in soon, + 17x7.5 MiM Venezias wheels +  225/45/17 and 235/40/17's ---------------Also have a 1996 3800 Series II 25k MOTOR FOR SALE.||||reg||1
